00:24:30.900 --> 00:24:31.700
Conservation

00:24:31.700 --> 00:24:36.340
Commission wishes to submit the following recommendations regarding the application pending application

00:24:36.500 --> 00:24:37.780
I WWC

00:24:37.780 --> 00:24:39.300
20 dash 34

00:24:39.300 --> 00:24:42.580
for a plan timber harvest located at 110 Cone Road

00:24:43.155 --> 00:24:46.115
in late twenty twenty the Conservation Commission

00:24:46.195 --> 00:24:55.955
initiated a natural resource and inventory and mapping project. The first phase of this project is focused on mapping natural resources associated with parcels

00:24:56.280 --> 00:24:58.840
located within open space corridors

00:24:58.840 --> 00:25:01.720
designated in the town's 2015

00:25:01.880 --> 00:25:08.920
plan of conservation and development. The parcel at 110 Road falls within the Western Highlands open space corridor

00:25:09.635 --> 00:25:21.395
spans from the town's southern border with Hebron North through into Bolton. The quarter features several large interconnected parcels of land that support a diversity of forest and wetland habitats,

00:25:21.475 --> 00:25:24.275
including the state owned Bishop Swamp Wildlife Management Area.

00:25:24.790 --> 00:25:27.350
The natural resource inventory identified

00:25:27.350 --> 00:25:29.350
potential and I underline potential

00:25:29.590 --> 00:25:33.430
vernal pools within the parcel located at 110 Road Cone Road.

00:25:34.150 --> 00:25:44.865
Vernal pools in the forest surrounding them provide critical habitat for diversity of wildlife species and they are designated as a most important habitat in Connecticut's the state of Connecticut's

00:25:44.865 --> 00:25:45.825
2015

00:25:45.825 --> 00:25:47.825
wildlife conservation action plan.

00:25:48.385 --> 00:25:55.105
Three species of amphibians that require vernal pool habitat for breeding are also recognized of conservation importance in this plan.

00:25:56.170 --> 00:26:02.090
The Conservation Commission is supportive of land use and management projects that promote biological diversity

00:26:02.170 --> 00:26:05.690
and other natural resource values, including forest management.

00:26:06.250 --> 00:26:12.985
Although the Commission recognizes that timber harvests are permitted as a right of activity under Connecticut's general statutes,

00:26:13.145 --> 00:26:16.265
the commission wishes to submit the following recommendations

00:26:16.345 --> 00:26:19.545
to encourage the application of best development practices

00:26:19.545 --> 00:26:25.310
or best management practices, I should say, associated with vertical pools that may occur on the parcel.

00:26:25.630 --> 00:26:26.350
The

00:26:26.510 --> 00:26:28.990
first recommendation is that the

00:26:29.150 --> 00:26:32.670
we looked at the looked at the mapping

00:26:32.670 --> 00:26:35.390
and it was unclear whether there was any

00:26:35.475 --> 00:26:36.755
surveys

00:26:36.755 --> 00:26:45.475
done to identify vernal pools. So the first is conduct a field inspection determine if vernal pools are present and if present flag them in the field.

00:26:46.275 --> 00:26:53.580
However, the natural resource source inventory identified the presence of potential pools based on aerial

00:26:53.580 --> 00:26:55.180
photographic interpretation.

00:26:55.340 --> 00:26:58.460
To confirm a vernal pool, it really requires

00:26:58.940 --> 00:27:02.860
an assessment of the biological community. That's how they're defined in Connecticut,

00:27:03.605 --> 00:27:08.645
which typically occurs during the spring or summer activity season for the majority of species.

00:27:09.045 --> 00:27:16.085
However, examination of potential pools during the winter when there is no snow cover can often be a reliable indicator

00:27:16.590 --> 00:27:20.270
and timing is also critical in order to complete timber harvesting

00:27:20.270 --> 00:27:25.950
prior to the onset of the spring breeding season, which generally starts in early to mid March in Connecticut.

00:27:26.750 --> 00:27:27.470
The

00:27:27.710 --> 00:27:35.605
following best if there are vernal pools that were identified on potential vernal pools on the site, the following best management practices are recommended

00:27:35.685 --> 00:27:37.125
around those pools.

00:27:41.685 --> 00:27:45.685
Forestry operations should ideally occur during the period November through February

00:27:45.860 --> 00:27:48.820
outside of the activity season for the breeding species

00:27:48.980 --> 00:27:51.300
when and when the ground is typically frozen.

00:27:52.100 --> 00:27:57.140
Dry periods in July and August provide a secondary option outside of the winter season.

00:27:57.700 --> 00:28:00.980
Operations during the period March through June should be

00:28:01.220 --> 00:28:01.540
avoided.

00:28:02.715 --> 00:28:03.675
Ideally,

00:28:03.675 --> 00:28:10.395
the goal would be to leave forest habitat including ground cover intact with a zone approximately 100 feet around

00:28:11.115 --> 00:28:12.075
pool.

00:28:12.235 --> 00:28:18.960
If selective cuts are planned in this zone, they should avoid the use of heavy machinery and a minimum of 75%

00:28:18.960 --> 00:28:23.760
canopy cover should be allowed to remain standing within that 100 foot zone.

00:28:24.400 --> 00:28:30.095
Avoid any disturbance within the basin of the pool, including dropping trees or felling trees within that basin.

00:28:30.975 --> 00:28:43.270
Paul Minimize soil disturbance and avoid the creation of ruts throughout the area depressions or ruts created along access roads can fill with water and often act as decoys accepting breeding amphibians

00:28:43.510 --> 00:28:49.430
if depressions or ruts are created that may hold water, they should be filled with the completion of the operation.

00:28:49.590 --> 00:28:50.710
PAUL

00:28:50.710 --> 00:29:02.045
Use existing access roads and avoid locating any new roads near the pool and graveling of access roads should be avoided to prevent creating barriers for movement of amphibians across the landscape.

00:29:03.325 --> 00:29:07.325
Employ erosion and standard erosion sediment controls as necessary

00:29:07.405 --> 00:29:09.725
in areas of disturbance around pools.

00:29:09.965 --> 00:29:13.060
However, it is critical critical to to remove remove any any

00:29:13.380 --> 00:29:16.020
sediment and erosion fencing or other barriers

00:29:16.100 --> 00:29:20.500
that are employed once the operation is complete and the site is stabilized

00:29:20.580 --> 00:29:22.980
to once again provide prevent barriers.

00:29:23.300 --> 00:29:23.780
And finally,

00:29:24.255 --> 00:29:33.135
maintain coarse woody debris such as logs throughout the area to provide cover and consider leaving limbs and tops where felt they can provide some habitat.

00:29:33.295 --> 00:29:34.015
So

